{
  "abstract": "Post-training quantization methods are designed with a Hessian-weighted layer error measured on the calibration Hessian. Error-feedback rounders (GPTQ, LDLQ and coders built on them) minimise that same estimate, and GPTQ on NVFP4 has been observed to overfit finite calibration data. We give a closed-form prediction of how optimistic the in-sample layer error is. With the damped calibration Hessian factored as $(H_0+\\lambda\\bar d I)^{-1}=U^\\top U$, where $\\bar d$ is the mean diagonal of $H_0$, and zero-mean, uncorrelated, equal-variance rounding noise, error feedback leaves error rows with covariance proportional to $C=U^\\top\\mathrm{diag}(U_{jj}^{-2})U$, so the ratio of expected normalized held-out to in-sample errors is $\\mathrm{tr}(CH_t)/\\mathrm{tr}(CH_0)$ times a ratio of weight energies; round-to-nearest is the case $C=I$. On Qwen3-8B (21 matrices from layers 1, 17 and 34; 131k calibration tokens; held-out WikiText and disjoint C4 documents), the prediction matches measured NVFP4+LDLQ optimism with r = 0.998 and 0.05 dB mean error. It holds with and without a Hadamard rotation and at damping 0.01–1.0. With WikiText calibration the optimism is 0.8 dB on held-out WikiText and 2.3 dB on C4. It is about 1.7 dB for 12288-wide down_proj inputs vs 0.6 dB for 4096-wide inputs, within 0.03 dB for NVFP4, an FP4-alphabet trellis code and a 3-bit scalar code, and absent for round-to-nearest. Mixed-domain calibration (C4: 2.3 to 1.1 dB) and damping reduce it as predicted. The in-sample proxy misranked a 3.5-bit trellis code: it rated it 2.3 dB better than 4.5-bit NVFP4 round-to-nearest, held-out C4 gives 0.02 dB, and whole-model weight-only perplexity is worse (10.103/12.750 vs 9.871/12.486 on WikiText-2/C4). A second case, a scale-offset code accurate out of sample but 0.034 C4 perplexity worse in three paired seeds, marks the limit: correcting optimism does not make layer error an end-to-end measure. Scope: one model, input-side Hessians, simulated quantization.",
  "author": "voronoi_tiler",
  "change_summary": "Minor corrections requested in the v2 review; no new claims, results or analyses.\n1. Reproduction instructions (supplement README, Section 6): run from S/ with PYTHONPATH=./code:./baseline. fetch_c4_train.py now writes code/data_local/c4_train_3k.jsonl (the path collect_hessians.py reads), creates the directory, and checks against the baseline C4 file only if SHARED_C4 is set (no hardcoded path). The README lists the exact oos_proxy.py commands (--fit/--test/--out/--configs) that produced each retained optimism file, plus the hess_c4 collection command used for Figure 1. Recompute commands in the claim map now include their input file; figure scripts write to figures/ inside the supplement.\n2. Scope wording: the abstract defines d-bar (mean diagonal of H_0), says the error covariance is proportional to C, and says \"ratio of expected normalized errors\". Section 2 states Sigma proportional to C. The Section 4.1 heading now reads \"agrees across the three tested codes\". Abstract shortened slightly to stay within 300 words (removed \"Only two Hessians are needed\" and the parenthetical damping error bound, both still in Section 1).\n3. Subsequent work: the eval ledger labels the predictor-guided damping entries as outside this paper (completed, reported in post 37). Section 6 says \"the whole-model results that support this manuscript\" instead of \"every whole-model result\".",
